title The enclosure that named a sixth of itself -- three roster reds, then the jail's own twenty-one default mounts measured and declared
prompt Open as HUSH under the INNER LOOP Standing block -- roster cold then hot, reds first as standfast, QA on touch, the council rota, prose witnesses on prose sends, stamp-and-name, ASCII-first, READ SCOPE -- pick up the running thread, and take the Pond lane under the accrete-only gate boundary.
think ListAgents named no reachable peer, so I read the process table instead, which REDS %291 asks for. Two jails stand. Dream runs Codex on /home/keeper/grain-dream, its own clone, started 21:37:05 -- a peer on a different checkout, so no file of mine is his. The second is an ai-jail from Wed Aug 19 bound to /home/keeper/grain, this checkout, holding a jailed Claude Code: nine days and four hours elapsed and zero CPU seconds consumed, which is a parked session rather than a second hand. I am unjailed in tmux, my pid namespace the host's own, which is the Hush seat exactly as the card describes it.
think The cold roster ran 83 guards in about forty minutes and answered guards_red=2, tree_moved=no. Standfast: those close before any new work. Reading their evidence turned two reds into three, because one of the repairs was to roster a guard nobody had ever run, and its first hearing was red.
think The width red first. corpus_flagged_files read 362 against a ceiling of 359, and the four lines that carried it are worth naming because none of them is authored width. tools/rye/objc_seam.rye:6 is a doc comment saying the word usize in prose. objc_seam.rye:42 and macos_window_probe.rye:136 declare `pub extern fn CGBitmapContextCreate(... width: usize ...)`, which is CoreGraphics taking size_t -- a Rye declaration of it MUST read usize or the call is wrong at the ABI. And skate_grid.rye:124 indexes `[0..n_usize]`, where the five letters sit inside an identifier some earlier @intCast already widened.
think So the meter was the thing that had to move, and it could not be gamed instead: the only way to make an extern C declaration pass the elder filter is to write @as(usize somewhere on the line, which is writing to the meter rather than to the truth. count_authored gains three filters beside the two it had -- skip comment lines, treat `extern fn` as the inherited-C seam by the same argument that seats @intCast, and match the whole word. Measured over the same 1,899 sources: 362/1,369 becomes 329/1,263, and the 33 files and 106 lines between them were never authored width (48 comment lines, 6 extern declarations, 52 identifier-only). Both ceilings fall to the sharper reading. The gated DECLARED roster reads zero under both filters, so nothing this sharpening does can loosen the wall that refuses.
think The reach red next: unreached=1115 against 1113. Three witnesses had landed that evening with no roster row and no choir -- counsel_census, tally_bud, waymark_rung_drift. I ran all three before rostering any, because a row that seats a red guard is a row that hands the next lap a wall. Two were green. waymark_rung_drift was RED, at 17,249 against a ceiling of 17,248: a guard written for %329 four hours earlier, unheard because nothing ran it.
think Its two marks. A `ZETA`-plus-digit mark had entered a living Rye doc comment in the macOS grid probe. The other stood in construction/ITINERARY.md's Standing block, inside the very sentence teaching that counters are retired -- the law spelling the shape it forbids. The mark law's own clause on illustration decides that one: an example in prose is a SHAPE, so the card carries an `<N>` placeholder now. The door row naming the granted door keeps its number, since it names a booked thing rather than illustrating a form.
think Then the header I wrote to explain the catch spelled both marks in their counter form, and the reading went back up by two. A scan is a living tracked file, so a guard that names what it caught in the shape it refuses raises its own count. That is the law being exactly as literal as it promised, and the paragraph reads in placeholders now for the same reason the card does.
think The Pond lane's own door was the record. pond/enclosure_policy.kyri says of itself that it reads what the enclosure builds, and its sibling guard pond_policy_launcher says honestly in its own header that it compares what the launcher SPELLS. So I asked the enclosure. `ai-jail --dry-run` prints the bwrap argument vector without starting anything: run in a scratch directory with the flags agent-jail.sh spells by default, every mount is a jail default except the two the invocation creates. Twenty-one defaults; the record declared six of the twenty-seven mounts standing.
think Three of the twenty-one could not be written with the eight marks the module had. `--dev` and `--proc` raise NEW kernel filesystems, so `map /proc` would say the enclosure hands back the host's process table when it does the opposite -- that is `fresh`. Four tmpfs mounts lie over paths the plan also binds, hiding what is under them rather than offering scratch -- that is `mask`, and the rule is read off the plan itself (a tmpfs under a bound target) rather than off a roster of four paths somebody typed. And three ro-binds place a file the enclosure generated at a path inside its own walls -- that is `graft`, judged on where it stands, since nothing of the host's crosses.
obs The record now reads REFUSE, place=27 hold=2 refuse=2, naming `map /sys`. That is the module working rather than a regression, the same shape as `network on` holding a week earlier. Two lines refuse and both are real: the enclosure read-only-binds the host's whole /sys, which this module's roster refuses by name because it hands back what the namespaces were raised to hold; and it binds /tmp/.X11-unix read-write, the host X11 socket directory, because the launcher passes no --no-display. `device /dev/shm` holds, as every device outside the seated GPU lane does.
obs The seat I found and did not take. Four `mask` lines close the classic corners under that /sys map -- firmware, the security and debug interfaces, the fuse control. A reading that weighed a mask against the map above it would be a real improvement AND a loosening, and the quest's custody line puts any weakening of an isolation boundary at check-in. So the record says refuse out loud and the softening waits for a word. A lap that finds a wall does not get to lower it.
obs What the instrument is, said plainly, because it is the durable half. tools/fixtures/p/pond_enclosure_built_scan.sh reads a plan -- pinned, or live from a dry run -- normalizes it in four rules so no host name reaches the tree, drops the two invocation rows, translates each bwrap row into one declaration by a table written once, and gates undeclared_builds at zero. The pinned plan is TESTIMONY, a reading on a named host at a named stamp, so a clone with no jail still reads the gate; where the jail IS installed the live plan is read too and any disagreement is reported as plan_drift. On this pier, tonight, plan_drift=0.
obs The control caught its own author twice, which is the whole reason to write one. It expected declared_unbuilt=1 where the pen's own record carried a second line, and I had counted 26 behaviors where 29 print. Both were my arithmetic, not the scan's.
obs I fabricated a stamp and the tree caught it. I wrote 20260828.234500 into four roster rows and the card while the one clock read 20260828.233803 -- seven minutes into the future, which the naming law forbids outright. Corrected to 20260828.233813 everywhere in one pass, including the module's version constant, and the binary rebuilt so it reports the stamp it was built at. Reading the clock costs one command and guessing it costs a law.
obs And a symlink died under sed -i. tools/rye/skate_grid.rye is a symlink to brushstroke/skate_grid.rye; `sed -i` REPLACED the link with a regular file, which git named as `T` -- mode 120000 to 100644. The exec-bit rule's table says sed -i preserves the mode, and that is true for a regular file and silently false for a link, whose mode IS the link. Restored with git checkout, the edit reapplied to the real file, and the reading fell by two rather than one -- git ls-files lists a symlink and its target as two paths and grep follows the link, so one mark in one file was being read twice. That inflates a ratchet and can never hide a mark, which is the safe direction, so it is written into the scan's header rather than repaired tonight.
obs Sound's line came off the card by measurement rather than by assumption: the three broken links in tools/fixtures/r/radiant_pool_scope.md all resolve now, checked one by one against the filesystem.
obs The council rota read row 4, Earth and Mercury, the concrete. N is git rev-list --count HEAD at 3,684 and 3,684 mod 5 is 4; the last lap to record a row read 3, so this advances rather than repeats and no hand advance was owed. Its threshold page asks for the fact taken in whole at the door, before interpretation, and says the concrete fact outranks the plausible story every time -- which is the entire lap: the enclosure's own printed plan outranked a record that had described it from memory since the day it was written. Its cardinal is the one-clock law, which caught my fabricated stamp within the hour; its dual is the marked value, whose clause on new marks accreting is exactly the licence the three new declaration marks ride on.
obs QA on touch, at molt time: nine artifacts read, every one A+ 98 at meter setting -- the two new scans, the control, the witness, the pinned plan, the record, the two repaired meters, and the roster. No frame pushed.
obs The reds could not be booked, and that is the round's standing gate. construction/REDS.md sits at 24,571 of 24,576 with every row OPEN, so reds_fold.sh has nothing lawful to fold and the pre-commit wall refuses a new row. %335 never landed for that reason and these three cannot either. Their three fields are above, in this log, which is where the account belongs -- yet a red that lives only in a log is a red the ledger's own monotone guard cannot see.
